      "dom_id": "question-3986",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $6{y}^{3}+50{y}^{2}+100y=5{y}^{2}+25y$, then $y=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(y)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{3}+50{y}^{2}+100y & =5{y}^{2}+25y \\\\ 6{y}^{3}+45{y}^{2}+100y & =25y \\\\ 6{y}^{3}+45{y}^{2}+75y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3y.$ We can factor out the $3y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{3}+45{y}^{2}+75y & =0 \\\\ 3y(2{y}^{2}+15y+25) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{y}^{2}+15y+25$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y(2{y}^{2}+15y+25) & =0 \\\\ 3y(2y+5)(y+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $y=0,$ $y=-\\frac{5}{2},$ and $y=-5.$",
