      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(y)=6{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}-45{y}^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(y)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n6{y}^{4}+3{y}^{3}-45{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3{y}^{2}.$ We can factor out the $3{y}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}(2{y}^{2}+y-15) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{y}^{2}+y-15$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}(2{y}^{2}+y-15) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}(y+3)(2y-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $y=0,$ $y=-3,$ and $y=\\frac{5}{2}.$",
